1266:
A. Once the proposed improvements are substantially in place and the developer/subdivider is seeking final approval from the City, the developer/subdivider shall submit to the City planning and zoning department five copies of the final plat for review and comment. The final plat shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat as approved by the City. The plat shall include those portions of the preliminary plat, which have been developed and/or approved, provided that it complies with all requirements of this Appendix.
B. The developer/subdivider shall submit with all final plat submittals, soil data, which shall have been prepared by a qualified soil scientist that is approved by the board of health. The soil data should include test results for all proposed (septic tank) lots for which final plat approval is requested. Soil study test results shall be reviewed and certified by the Henry County Health Department. A report from the county health department indicating the status of each lot's capability to be developed.
C. When the final plat has been reviewed and approved by the planning and zoning department, the developer/subdivider shall supply one original drawing of the final plat on mylar or sepia in black ink, as well as 17 copies of the plat in blue line format. A licensed professional engineer or landscape architect shall prepare the plat. The minimum size of the plat(s) shall be 8½ inches x 11 inches and a maximum size of 17 inches x 22 inches. In addition, all other necessary documentation for recording must be submitted at this time. Both Zoning Administrator and mayor shall sign the final plat. The final plat shall than be presented to the City Council as an update to them of development activities occurring in the City.
D. The developer shall be responsible for properly recording the final plat with the Clerk of Superior Court of Henry County. The developer shall then submit to the City, confirmation (letter, receipt, signed plat, etc.) from the Superior Court, that the plat has been recorded. The mylar and three copies of the plat will be returned to the developer, with one copy going to the following city/county departments:
1. Two copies to the City planning and zoning department;
2. One copy to the county planning and zoning department;
3. One copy to the County Engineer;
4. Two copies to the City utility department;
5. One copy to the county health department;
6. One copy to the county D.O.T.;
7. One copy to the county tax assessor's department;
8. One copy to the county board of education;
9. One copy to the county 911 office;
10. One copy to the City police department;
11. One copy to the county G.I.S. department;
12. One copy to the county voters registration; and
13. One copy to the postmaster general.
E. The planning and zoning department shall retain remaining copies of the final plat.
F. Other important items necessary for consideration of final plat approval by the planning and zoning department shall consist of:
1. Completion of public improvements. The developer shall contact the planning and zoning department for a checklist specifying the procedure in obtaining the various approvals from the City, which will release the subdivision or final plat consideration.
2. Bonds and/or letters of credit required. The developer/subdivider may be required, in the discretion of the Zoning Administrator, the following bonds and/or letters of credit:
i. Bond defined. For the purposes of this section, the term "bond" shall mean a performance guaranty given in form satisfactory to the Zoning Administrator and obtained from a surety licensed to do business in the State of Georgia and named in the current list of "Companies Holding Certificates of Authority as Acceptable Sureties on Federal Bonds and as Acceptable Reinsuring Companies," as published in Circular 570 (amended) by the Financial Management Service, Surety Bond Branch. U.S. Department of the Treasury. All bonds signed by an agent must be accompanied by a certified copy of such agent's authority to act.
ii. Letter of credit defined. For the purposes of this section, the term "letter of credit" shall mean an irrevocable letter of credit given in form satisfactory to the Zoning Administrator and drawn upon a banking or other financial institution licensed to do business in the State of Georgia. At a minimum, the letter of credit shall provide for the immediate and unconditional drawing of any or all of the amount of the letter upon presentment and demand for payment.
iii. Maintenance bond/letter of credit. The developer/subdivider shall be required to post either a bond or, in the sole discretion of the Zoning Administrator, a letter of credit in an amount at least equal to that necessary to cover the cost of infrastructure improvements upon neglect or abandonment of the project.
iv. Residential bond/letter of credit. The amount of the maintenance bond/letter of credit for a residential development shall not be less than $100.00 per lineal foot of street and $10.00 per lineal foot of sidewalks within the development.
v. Nonresidential bond/letter of credit. The amount of the maintenance bond/letter of credit for a nonresidential development shall not be less than $100.00 per lineal foot of street within the development.
vi. Landscape bond/letter of credit. The maintenance bond/letter of credit shall state, in addition to all other amounts listed in this section, sufficient funding available to cover the cost of required landscaping or vegetating a site of development. Tree replacement, ground cover replacement, or buffer replacement shall be the responsibility of the developer/subdivider in addition to the required landscape plan provided for in the zoning ordinance. The additional amount of the bond/letter of credit shall be set by the Zoning Administrator or, in his absence, by the City Manager. Additional costs to the City outside of the amount of the bond shall be charged to the developer/subdivider and shall constitute a lien upon the property.
vii. Duration of bond/letter of credit. The maintenance bond/letter of credit required by this section shall have an initial duration of three years and shall be renewed, in the sole discretion of the City Council, for additional terms of three years. Except as otherwise provided in this section, upon final inspection and certification from the City that all infrastructure work covered under the bond is satisfactorily completed, in good condition and in compliance with all applicable laws, ordinances, rules, regulations, and construction standards, the maintenance bond/letter of credit shall be released.
3. For developments with multiple phases of construction, separate maintenance bonds/letters of credit may be accepted by the Zoning Administrator for each phase. However, where improvements in one phase of a development connect or are integrated into another phase, the maintenance bond(s)/letter(s) of credit given on one, multiple, or all phases of development may, when, in the sole discretion of the Zoning Administrator and/or the City Council it is necessary, be renewed until certification from the City that all infrastructure work within the development as a whole is satisfactorily completed, in good condition and in compliance with all applicable laws, ordinances, rules, regulations and construction standards.
(Ord. No. 457, § 1, 8-14-18)

1271:
The process for approving an individual residential plat (only), that is not part of a subdivision. All applicants shall include the following information on all individual plat submittals. The planning and zoning department shall check the plat(s) for accuracy of other city/county mapping and ordinance compliance. The following information shall be included on each plat/survey:
A. Zoning of property; including setback information;
B. Correct land lot(s) and district(s);
C. Scale of measurement (preferably; 1": 100' or 1": 50');
D. Outline of planning and zoning foot prints;
E. Notes which indicate if property(s) is in a 100-year floodplain, wetland and/or watershed area or if any other bodies of water are on property(s) and indicate on property;
F. Dimensions and acreage of property;
G. Proposed and existing easements and setbacks on-site;
H. Contours at two- or five-foot intervals;
I. Tree save areas;
J. North arrow;
K. Engineer's/surveyor's seal, signature, phone number, address;
L. Iron pins set at all corners unless otherwise noted;
M. A 20-foot drainage easement and sanitary easement shown inside all rear lot lines and all easements shown on side and/or front lot lines;
N. Calculations which demonstrate that the development, as a whole, complies with the 25 percent impervious cover requirements of this Appendix;
O. Note: City of Hampton, Georgia assumes no responsibility for overflow or erosion of natural or artificial drains beyond the extent of the street right-of-way; nor for the extension of culverts beyond the point shown on this plat;
P. All storm drains shall be extended a minimum of 30 feet behind future residence;
Q. The field data upon which this plat is based has a closure precision of at least one foot in 100,000 feet;
R. This plat has been calculated for closure and is accurate within at least one foot in 100,000 feet;
S. All lot lines measured to hundredths (0.01) of a foot. The bearings shall be indicated for all lot lines to the nearest second. The error of closure should not be less than one foot in 100,000 feet;
T. Stipulation of deed restrictions and/or covenants on the property;
U. The lengths of all arcs, including radii, points of curvature, tangent bearings, and the length and bearing of the long cord of every arc;
V. Restrictive covenants and/or conditions of zoning; and
W. These and all other standards as described in Section 3-9 of this Appendix shall apply to the approval of individual plats.
(Ord. No. 457, § 1, 8-14-18)

1339:
This Article applies to the following activities:
(a) New development that creates or adds 5,000 square feet or greater of new impervious surface area or that involves land disturbing activity of 1 acre of land or greater;
(b) Redevelopment (excluding routine maintenance and exterior remodeling) that creates, adds, or replaces 5,000 square feet of greater of new impervious surface area of that involves land disturbing activity of 1 acre or more;
(c) New development and redevelopment if
(i) such new development or redevelopment is part of a subdivision or other common plan of development, and
(ii) the sum of all associated impervious surface area or land disturbing activities that are being developed as part of such subdivision or other common plan of development meets or exceeds the threshold in (a) and (b) above;
(d) Any commercial or industrial new development or redevelopment, regardless of size, that is a hotspot land use as defined in this Article; and
(e) Linear transportation projects that exceed the threshold in (a) or (b) above.
(Ord. No. 534, §§ 1, 2, 1-12-21)

1340:
